 was very unhappy America was  supporting India and B he therefore  wanted to be compensated as he liked to  say for Pakistan's neutrality and  compensation meant give me Kashmir  Kennedy refused to give in to this  blackmail and he sent repeated messages  to the Pakistanis that if you enter this  war we will regard you as part of the  enemy not as part of the alliance that  we have with you the 20th of November  was sheer panic in New Delhi John  Kenneth Galbraith writes in his memoirs  that it looked like India was going to  disintegrate all Indian aircraft private  and public were commissioned  commissioned by the Indian Air Force  martial law was declared in much of  northeast India and India belatedly  began moving troops from the Pakistan  front even though it feared a second  front could open up and then that night  for no reclamation China unilaterally  stopped the Chinese ceased their  offensive and announced that within a  month they would draw back to the lines  where they had started we don't know why  China did that Chinese records are not  available today you can get a lot of  records about what the American  government thought about what the Indian  government thought but Chinese archives  remain sealed Nehru credited John F  Kennedy with convincing the Chinese to  stop he told John Kenneth  Galbraith that it was America's resolve  and Kennedy's determination not to let  the Chinese win manifested in the  airlift and manifested in the deployment  of the aircraft carrier battle group  they're persuaded Beijing to stop and  not let this crisis continue to go any  further this of course leaves us with  the great what if what if the Chinese  hadn't stopped that night would the  United States have found itself at war  with Communist China  remember in 1962 it would only been nine  years before when we had been at war  with Communist China and Korea fought a  war in Korea with the Communist Chinese  for the better part of three years and  here Nehru was essentially asking us to  go to war with China again we'll never  know  it's a what-if but in my judgment  Kennedy almost certainly would have said  yes and I make that judgment for several  reasons first because he really did  believe that Indian democracy was  crucial to the United States in the  global balance of power second because  his ambassador John Kenneth Galbraith  who was a personal friend and one of the  people closest to the president would  almost certainly have recommended it and  third because just a year later in the  fall of 1963 the United States Air Force  the Royal Air Force and small squadrons  from the Royal Australian Air Force and  the Royal Canadian Air Force deployed  into India for a military training  exercise which was exactly what Nehru  had asked for a year before so we  actually practiced going into this war  the conflict between China and India  ended in a ceasefire unilaterally on the  21st of November 1962 but the conflicts  not over China and India have never  settled their border dispute this border  dispute remains the longest unsettled  border dispute in the world today  neither party agrees to wear that border  should be there have been hundreds of  meetings between the Chinese and Indians  and they haven't succeeded one iota  and moving forward they are big trading  partners with each other today the  likelihood of another China India war is  probably pretty low I wouldn't say the  same thing about the likelihood of  another india-pakistan war that I think  is probably a very serious reality so  that dispute continues to this day and  in addition that dispute has led to two  other things an access between China and  Pakistan China and Pakistan had barely  any interaction with each other before  1962 but if you look back in 50 years  perspective it's 1962 or the alliance  between China and Pakistan begins and  today that Alliance is very strong they  call it the all-weather alliance it's  kind of a snub at the United States  we're not all whether they are all  whether they say it's taller than the  Himalayas and deeper than the Indian  Ocean and just this year China and  Pakistan signed agreements which will  lead to 46 billion dollars in Chinese  investment in Pakistani infrastructure  and of course it heads another dimension  their secret nuclear collusion Pakistan  and China have been secret nuclear  partners since the 1960s China gave  Pakistan the designs for its nuclear  weapons force and today Pakistan has the  fastest growing a nuclear weapons  program in the world faster than any  other country this has also led to a  triangular  arms race probably the most dangerous  arms race in the world today between  China and Pakistan on the one hand and  India on the other just two years ago  India proudly announced that had fired  an intermediate-range ballistic missile  that allowed it for the first time to  target Beijing with nuclear weapons so  the crisis that was averted in 1962  remains a problem even to this day  john f kennedy's forgotten store crisis  is the story of that crisis mostly from  the American point of view it also has  several other backstories which I'd be  happy to talk about in Q&amp;A like the role  played by the first lady who's very  important in this whole issue and the  role played by the CIA in trying to  sponsor a rebellion in Tibet but let me  just finish with one final word about  sources this book relies upon letters  that have only been Declassified in the  last couple of years between Kennedy and  Nehru for more than 50 years the Indian  government denied these letters even  existed  because in some ways they're very very  embarrassing India at its moment of  greatest need had to ask the United  States to bail it out the letters are  declassified and this is the first book  that takes advantage of looking at those  documents in depth with that thank you  very much for your attention and I'd be  delighted to take questions if I can  come up with answers 