 to the White House  that sadar was to be given VIP treatment  flown home on its air own airplane and  there was going to be nothing about  these silly rules about checking the  veterinary background to this horse was  to be shipped immediately to her farm  well it is the DAR that bride's  riderless behind john f kennedy's  caisson during the funeral of  Pennsylvania Avenue and over to  Arlington Park it's kind of a unique  reminder of the role india and pakistan  played in the in the kennedy  administration the other thing she did  of course was she was a hostess she  hosted both Ayub Khan and Nehru for  state visits to the United States the  state visit for field marshal Ayub Khan  was unique only once in American history  has Mount Vernon George Washington's  home been used for a state visit Jackie  wanted to use Mount Vernon she had just  come back from a trip with her husband  to Versailles in France and the  Schonbrunn palace in Austria but we  don't have any palaces but she wanted to  do something equally grand so in June  1961 Mount Vernon was the site of a very  special summit between Ayub Khan and  John F Kennedy a few months later Nehru  came it was in November and Nehru they  had now of course if you'd had Mount  Vernon for the Pakistani what do you do  to outdo that  so they took Nehru to Jackie's home in  Newport  Rhode Island Hammersmith farms it's  called a cottage but if you've ever  driven around Newport you'll realize  their idea of a cottage is a lot  different than most of us but it turned  out to be a complete failure nehru at  this point was getting on in age he was  very tired he was very exhausted from  the flight he answered most of Kennedy's  requests and most of Kennedy's thoughts  with mono syllabic short answers there  was no engagement and then it got worse  after the stop and Newport they came  down to the White House to have official  dinner in the White House as well and  somebody on the White House staff failed  to properly open the flue in the  fireplace in the East Room and the  meeting began with smoke spreading  throughout the White House was not a  very auspicious starting to their  relationship but mrs. Kennedy in this  regard was was it was a crucial partner  with her husband in the American  engagement with both India and Pakistan  in the early 1960s  yeah please document I am wondering if  these documents shed any light on the  CIA involvement in Tibet and on the GA  support for the khumba's in their  guerrilla fight against the Chinese sure  let me back up just a little bit in the  1950s Pakistan was America who's called  America's most allied Ally we actually  had more treaties of alliance with  Pakistan than we did with any other  country in the world Pakistan was a  member of the central treaty  organization was a member of the  Southeast Asia Treaty Organization we  were firmly in bed with the Pakistan's  the Eisenhower administration liked  Pakistan because it was clearly with us  in the Cold War whereas India was a much  more neutral posture the CIA as a result  developed a very close relationship with  its Pakistani counterpart in the 1950s  one of these relationships was the  famous you to a YouTube aircraft were  based in Peshawar in Pakistan that's  where they took off to fly over the  Soviet Union that's where the the famous  mission they were shot down began in  1959 there was a second secret operation  and that was to support the Tibetan  resistance in 1950 China moved troops  into Tibet the story of whether Tibet is  part of China or not part of China you  can go to the to a library there are  bookshelves full of books arguing both  sides of it I'm not gonna go into that  today from the Tibetan standpoint it was  pretty clear-cut their country was  invaded by the Chinese their way of life  was attacked by China in many ways and  their spiritual leader the Dalai Lama  was forced into exile or went into exile  what few people know is that when the  Dalai Lama went into e