ery very good  accounts of the operation written by CIA  officers who were involved in it and who  got their memoirs published cleared by  the CIA's review process which provide a  very very in-depth report on what was  going on just to go back to take you  back to Mountain Vernon for one more  minute the business between Kennedy and  Ayub Khan that night was top secret high  Yukon the month before the Mount Vernon  Senate had turned off use of the air  force base in East Pakistan he wanted to  send Kennedy a message if you want to  flirt with the Indians if you want to  give India economic assistance it's  gonna cost you something he didn't want  to do this in public he didn't want to  have a public falling-out so the easiest  way to send the message was to cut off a  covert program the Americans would know  the rest of the world wouldn't  necessarily know it wouldn't become a  big issue Kennedy at that dinner that  night in addition to a lot of Senators  and cabinet members also invited the  director of Central Intelligence Allen  Dulles and when everyone else was having  their mint juleps and orange juice  before dinner Kennedy took Ayub Khan for  a walk in the gardens around Mount  Vernon and asked him would you let us  continue the operation to fight the  Communist Chinese would you resume use  of the Tibet pace and either because of  the charm of the night or the charm of  the president or maybe because he always  intended to he agreed to and resumed the  assistance resumed that operation now  flash forward to Averell Harriman when  Averill Harriman arrives in India he has  three items on his agenda one is a  political agenda how to work between  India and Pakistan second is a military  agenda how much more military assistance  to get to the Indians what kind of  assistance what kind of joint training  operations to  we pursue and a covert agenda because  now that China and India had gone to war  the CIA and the White House judged the  Indians were ready to re